An API Gateway is a common entry point between the client and backend services.
--> Instead of
Client

|----> User
|----> Order
|----> Payment
|----> Product
--> We have
             Client
                |
                v
           API Gateway
           /    |    \
          /     |     \
         v      v      v
       User   Order  Payment

GET /api/users/1001
goes:
Client
↓
Gateway
↓
User Service

GET /api/orders/5001
goes:
Client
↓
Gateway
↓
Order Service

Gateways can later handle things such as:
1. Authentication
2. Rate limiting
3. Request filtering
4. Routing
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Service discovery helps one service find the current location of another service without knowing the exact IP address or port number.so,
Instead of saying : "Call 10.10.1.21:8081"
we say : "Find USER-SERVICE"
Order Service
      ↓
Service Discovery
      ↓
In which server is my user service
      ↓
User Service instance
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Database Ownership
Suppose:
User Service → User data
Order Service → Order data
Payment Service → Payment data
Then:
Order Service
      |
      X
      |
      | Don't directly query
      v
User Database
Instead:
Order Service
      |
      | API call
      v
User Service
      |
      v
User Database
--> Because User Service owns User data.
--> Not every Microservice need a separate physical database
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Many services
     ↓
Client complexity

API Gateway
     ↓
Common entry point


Many service instances
     ↓
Location problem
     ↓
Service Discovery


Many databases/data areas
     ↓
Ownership problem
     ↓
Each service owns its data
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--> A timeout does NOT make Payment Service faster it protects Order Service from waiting forever.
--> Retry means the request failed, try again(Useful when the failure is temporary).
Idempotency means : Doing the same request multiple times produces the same final result as doing it once.
--> Payment Service is down,Circuit breaker says:"Payment is clearly unhealthy. Stop calling it for a while."
Circuit Breaker States :
1. CLOSED
--> Everything is working normally.
Order → Circuit Breaker → Payment
--> Requests are allowed.
2. OPEN
--> Payment Service keeps failing.
Circuit Breaker opens:
Order → Circuit Breaker → Payment
--> Requests don't even reach Payment Service.Instead, you can immediately return:"Payment service temporarily unavailable"
3. HALF-OPEN
--> After some time, Circuit Breaker tests the service:

Circuit Breaker → Payment Service
                     ↓
                    Works?

If it works → CLOSED again.
If it fails → OPEN again.
--> Fallback means : What alternative response should we provide when the normal operation cannot complete?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Working of resilience patterns


Client
  ↓
API Gateway
  ↓
Order Service
  ↓
Payment Service

Timeout : Don't wait forever
Retry : Try again for temporary failures
Circuit Breaker : Stop repeatedly calling an unhealthy service
Fallback : Give the client a clear and truthful response

In one line:
Timeout → limit waiting
Retry → recover temporary failure
Circuit Breaker → stop repeated failure
Fallback → handle failure gracefully
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
